US President Donald Trump says he's  cancelled a secret meeting with the  Taliban in the United States leaders  were supposed to fly to Camp David on  Saturday night US Secretary of State  Mike Pompeo says talks with the Taliban  are now dead for the time being and the  special envoy is being recalled meetings  between him and the Taliban have been  going on in Qatar for nearly a year it  was the case that when the Taliban tried  to gain negotiating advantage by  conducting terror attacks inside of the  country president forum made the right  decision say that's not gonna work we're  gonna walk away from a deal if others  try to use violence to achieve better  ends in the negotiations not right it's  not appropriate it killed an American  and it made no sense for the Taliban to  be rewarded for that kind of bad  behavior well the Taliban has released a  statement in response saying Americans  will suffer more than anyone else  because of Trump's decision to cancel  the talks while the office of Afghan  President Ashraf Ghani says peace will  only be possible if the Taliban stop  launching attacks and hold direct talks  with the government we expected an  outcome that could lead us to a  ceasefire that could lead us to a direct  negotiation with the Afghan government  and the Taliban  so unfortunately so that's that's we  didn't see our current error a we saw  and we observed that there could be  risks to any process that the Afghan  government is not the main stakeholder  in that and any process that cannot  guarantee a ceasefire a halt to the  violence could be harmful for all of the  achievements that we've gained while  Tony Burley is standing by in Kabul but  first let's go to rob Reynolds in  washington DC Rob how is the news of  this cancellation playing out there well  certainly there was a lot of shock Rob  when President Trump tweeted on Saturday  that there had been this meeting planned  for Camp David Camp David of course is a  very historically significant place and  many wondered whether  the idea of having the Taliban  leadership go to meet with the president  there was appropriate but now that the  meeting is off people are saying that  the president made the right call at  least those within his his own inner  circle as you just heard from Secretary  of State Mike Pompeo he said that the  talks are dead for the time being he  gave no indication as to whether or when  they might restart secretary Pompeo also  warned that things are about to get  worse in his words for the Taliban after  the killing of a US soldier in a car  bomb attack in Kabul on Thursday now  members of the president's own party as  well as members of his inner circle  including the national security adviser  John Bolton had been dubious about the  talks with the Taliban they had been  counseling the president that the  Taliban's word could not be taken at  face value and that the organization  could not be trusted to keep its  promises but the president has made  promises of his own namely that he would  withdraw the remaining 14 thousand US  troops from Afghanistan not to do so or  to not be able to fulfill that pledge  could have political repercussions for  Trump and Trump has used this tactic  before Rob both as a businessman and as  president of walking away from a deal in  the final stages if he felt that he  could get a better position or  strengthened negotiating position by  doing so you will recall that in the  run-up to his first meeting with kim  jonghwan the north korean leader he  abruptly cancelled the talks and then  the talks were back on after a fairly  short time so it would not be surprising  I think for many in Washington to find  out that after a certain period of time  the talks were to resume but that is  completely up to the president Trump Rob  for now thanks very much indeed I want  to go to Tony Berkeley who's with  from Kabul obviously the Afghan  government has essentially been  sidelined for many of the talks that  Rob's been talking about there what's  the reaction been there well in public  they're very supportive of the American  peace process in private they have been  very critical but today they're  welcoming this move by the US president  they've always said that they should be  party to these talks  the Taliban refused to talk directly to  the Afghan government they consider them  puppets of the Americans and  illegitimate and corrupt ironically the  the way this is all played out that the  Taliban said that they were close to an  agreement whereby they were going to  start talking with the Afghan government  on September the 23rd they perhaps have  been the most surprised by this tweet  from Donald Trump because they were  expecting this draft agreement to be a  done deal they said they were talking on  Saturday with mr. Khalilzad the chief US  negotiator and according to a statement  they've released today that they said  that both sides were in total agreement  the agreement was finalized  international regional countries had  been in agreement with this and they  they were very surprised that this has  been stopped at this stage they positive  they tweeted positive statements on  Saturday so this has come as a shock to  them but they also said in their  statement that America as you said  before would suffer more than they would  now that's anyone's guess what that's  going to mean there's two choices facing  the Taliban now do they go away escalate  the conflict or do they come back to the  negotiating table at some point in a  different frame of mind we find a wait  and see which frame of mind that will be  Tony thanks very much indeed let's talk  to David said me he's a senior associate  at the Center for Strategic and  International Studies he's also a former  US Deputy Assistant Secretary of Defense  for Afghanistan Pakistan and Central  Asia he's joining us on skype from Kabul  thank you very much indeed sir for  giving us your time one watching from  the outside would get the sense that  there was we were reaching a significant  moment in these talks  if these talk this secret meeting was  supposed to be held at Camp David do you  think given your diplomatic background  sir that the president  reaction and response has been  proportionate I would say that certainly  from my perspective here in Kabul  talking to Afghans I think they're the  vast majority of people that I speak to  welcome this decision the fact that this  agreement was reached earlier in the  week and then followed by a wave of  attacks on Afghan cities in the north  and the west of the country and then by  a brutal bombing here in Kabul sent the  message to Afghans that the Taliban were  not interested in peace their focus was  on military victory and that the US  agreement was a withdrawal agreement not  a peace agreement so from that  perspective I think there is clearly a  lot of work to be done the Taliban are  claiming that US will suffer but in fact  it's Afghan civilians who are suffering  the most they're the ones who are being  killed every day by the Taliban and in  crossfire between the Taliban and  government forces but by and large I  think most Afghans think this is the  right thing many would love to have seen  peace but they didn't think the  agreement that the u.s. brought was  going to be a peace agreement we heard  our own US Secretary of State Mike  Pompeo saying that the deal was  essentially dead for now what does it  take do you think to try to get this  process back on the road again I think  it will take really a mind change a  mindset change on the part of the  Taliban they have to show that they want  peace they are rebuilt they are willing  to stop using violence if they do that  then the talks can resume but right now  again this the very direct bunch of  signals the actions for the Taliban over  the last week have been exactly the  opposite that they're interested in  military victory not in peace and how  that's going to play out inside the  Taliban is a difficult question give us  some context on that sir if you would  how much of a disconnect is there  between the Taliban members who are  going through the negotiation process  and the land are at the leadership and  the Taliban groups on the ground  perhaps outside the influences as well  who perhaps do not want this peace  process to work well I'm not inside the  Taliban so I can't say that with  certainty but it does appear that  there's a fairly large disconnect  between the Taliban negotiators and what  the Taliban commanders were doing this  coordinated assault across northern  Western Afghanistan on on four  provincial capitals was not something  that they did on the spur of the moment  it had to have been carefully planned  carefully thought out a lot of logistics  put in place before these attacks took  place and it doesn't appear that the  existence of this agreement in Doha made  any difference to the military  commanders here so I think it's very  much an open question as to whether the  column on speak with one voice thank you  very much indeed for giving us your  analysis David said knee we appreciate  it thank you
 

